WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.067
The big take away

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.836
from this is very interesting.

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:05.238
They are outlining several steps

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:06.639
that many different bodies,

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.641
whether it's Congress,

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:11.644
bar associations, within different states

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:13.913
or law enforcement agencies, the steps

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.248
that they should take

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:16.116
as a result

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:17.951
of this comprehensive report

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:19.352
from this committee.

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.354
The biggest one that I'm seeing here

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:21.688
is that

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:24.090
this committee is recommending that

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:27.527
there be steps taken to enforce

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:30.063
the 14th Amendment section three.

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:30.997
This committee says,

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:31.564
of course, that

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.466
that part of the Constitution

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:34.868
says that anyone that incites

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.002
an insurrection

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.003
should be barred

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:38.738
from holding any future office.

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:39.472
So specifically,

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:40.173
it says

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:41.307
the committee believes that

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:42.375
those who took an oath

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:42.976
to protect

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:43.410
and defend

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.779
the Constitution on January 6th

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:47.180
should be barred,

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.515
disqualified and barred

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:50.450
from holding government office.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.818
And if you read between the lines

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:52.552
there, of course,

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:54.120
they are pointing directly

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:54.554
at the former

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:55.321
President Trump,

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.524
who has already announced his candidacy

00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:58.691
for 20, 24.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.760
So that's a big step from this committee

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.728
saying that look

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.163
it's right there in the Constitution.

00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:04.898
If you inciting an insurrection

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:06.433
you are barred from holding future

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:07.400
office.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.001
Unclear

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:09.836
if that would actually be implemented,

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:11.571
but the committee is urging that.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.473
And one other important thing

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:14.774
here, Laura, you know,

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:15.842
they're talking about

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:18.578
how law enforcement agencies

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:20.613
federal agencies, Secret

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:22.382
Service, really need to take a hard

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:24.484
look at violent extremism

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:25.351
because, of course,

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:28.388
that's part of what amplified

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:31.424
and sort of fed into this mob movement

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.493
that moved to the Capitol on January 6th,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.495
breached the Capitol, stormed

00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:37.530
inside, resulted in injuries,

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:38.565
even a few deaths

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:40.100
They're saying that law enforcement,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:40.834
other agencies

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:43.002
need to take a big look at that.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:43.970
And then, of course,

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:44.737
they're also saying,

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:46.139
oh, and here, Laura,

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:47.040
they're talking about

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.608
the electoral count act.

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:51.945
And of course, they want to codify that.

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:53.313
No vice president,

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:56.616
no other official can ever overthrow

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.218
the electoral count.

00:01:58.218 --> 00:02:00.353
The electors that have been chosen

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:01.621
via individual states.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:01.921
Of course,

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:02.922
that was something

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:04.591
that the former president was pushing

00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:05.992
Vice President Mike Pence to do.

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:06.426
It's something

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:07.293
ultimately that

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:09.362
the vice president refused to do.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:10.997
But the committee here

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:11.598
pointing out

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:13.166
that the House of Representatives

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:14.100
has passed

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.768
what's called the Presidential Election

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:16.736
Reform Act.

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:18.571
They are now urging the Senate to pass

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:19.606
that as well

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.307
so that it is codified

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:22.142
that the vice president

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:24.210
does not have any power in this process,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.044
which, of course,

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:25.445
sort of

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:29.516
was one of the initial initial points

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:30.617
that the former president

00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:32.285
tried to seize on

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:33.653
and urge the vice president

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:34.787
to overturn the election.

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.456
But a lot in here, Laura.

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:37.590
Most of it, though,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.858
the narrative here

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:41.194
and really expounding upon everything

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:43.396
they've shown in testimony,

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:44.397
in the public testimony,

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:46.099
putting it in black and white,

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:47.867
and then in these appendices

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:49.402
detailing the recommendations

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:51.070
that they are making to many bodies

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.572
about what can be done now.

00:02:52.572 --> 00:02:53.673
I want to bring in former U.S.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.775
attorney Harry Litman.

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:56.509
Harry, I'm

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:57.977
so glad that you're with us tonight.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:03:00.113
This is a very significant day.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:01.214
We've been waiting for this

00:03:01.214 --> 00:03:02.348
not just since Wednesday,

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:03.383
but really since, well,

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.451
probably January six when the committee

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:07.320
was actually impaneled

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.855
I'm wondering in terms of

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:09.989
you've seen recommendations,

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:11.191
there's about 11 of them

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:12.759
that goes beyond that initial

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:13.993
executive summary.

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:14.961
It includes things

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:16.329
like the Electoral Count Act.

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:17.163
That includes

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:18.498
accountability,

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:21.167
a deeper dove into violent extremism,

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:21.868
that includes

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.903
the national special security event

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:25.738
and the protocol that you do

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:27.240
to prevent this from happening.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.308
But one in particular

00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:29.609
that I'm really curious about,

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:31.678
your take as a former prosecutor

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:34.180
is this 14th Amendment Section three,

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:35.648
the one where frankly,

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:37.817
we recall the second impeachment

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.586
of the former president Donald Trump,

00:03:39.586 --> 00:03:42.188
a focus was on the disqualification

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:44.757
of somebody from holding office.

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.392
Now we're in the future.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:47.860
If the participated

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.128
or helped to incite

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:50.363
or give comfort

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:52.966
and aid to insurrectionists, for example.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:55.702
Tell me about this particular section.

00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:56.903
What stands out to you

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.204
about this recommendation?

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:00.273
The first

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:01.040
thing that stands

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:01.407
out is

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:04.077
the committee is actually going for it.

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:07.680
So Raskin made it clear in his remarks.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.782
So did the Vice Chair, Cheney.

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:12.552
It's a bit of a constitutional puzzle.

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:14.320
And I've thought about this

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.222
and studied it a lot.

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:17.190
How exactly

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.058
you effectuate this provision

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:21.060
in the 14th Amendment that says

00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:23.696
once you have sworn

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:24.964
an oath to the Constitution,

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:26.366
then committed insurrection

00:04:26.366 --> 00:04:28.735
and you may not serve in the future?

00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:30.270
It has to be.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.138
It has to be the case

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:34.907
that it can be brought into effect.

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.342
And yet

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:37.410
there's some law out there

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.512
that suggests Congress has to vote it.

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:41.180
There's some law that

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.115
that suggests

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:44.717
just a judge or a jury could find it.

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:46.152
I think

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:47.687
the committee wasn't sure,

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:50.623
but they really want to make this play.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:51.691
And you can see it,

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:55.361
the kind of grand compromise as to just

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:58.631
this large prompt from the body politic.

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:01.134
So the big thing is they're going for it

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:03.336
and they're remember that

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:05.004
just just this week.

00:05:05.004 --> 00:05:06.005
40 Republican

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:06.939
40 Democrats

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:08.941
in the House of Representatives

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:12.612
have done a motion to try to effectuate

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:14.147
just this.

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.382
Raskin is clearly the author of it.

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:18.551
He's the constitutional law professor.

00:05:18.885 --> 00:05:20.887
It must be able to be done.

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:22.989
But how exactly is a mystery?

00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:24.657
And they want to get it right

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:25.858
because the risk is

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.527
you'd go through all the steps

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:31.664
So that's something that really

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:33.066
jumped out at me, jumped out

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:35.034
even on Monday.

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:37.470
It's a really important point because

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.172
the idea of the proof

00:05:39.172 --> 00:05:40.640
and the going for it,

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:41.541
given the fact that I mean,

00:05:41.541 --> 00:05:43.943
on the one hand, Harry, it's great news.

00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:45.511
It's not a whole lot of precedent

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:47.013
on a case like this. Right.

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:48.781
You don't want there to be volumes

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:49.716
about case

00:05:49.716 --> 00:05:51.084
law that actually talks

00:05:51.084 --> 00:05:53.319
about people who were aiding and abetting

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:54.821
and or giving aid and comfort

00:05:54.821 --> 00:05:56.489
or actually fighting insurrection

00:05:56.489 --> 00:05:57.790
against our government.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.525
But because of that, in this case,

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:00.960
law and common law system

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:01.394
that we're in,

00:06:01.394 --> 00:06:03.329
when we look at former cases

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:04.731
and we decide how to go forward

00:06:04.731 --> 00:06:05.898
based on the former

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.600
holdings of the court,

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.002
it is a difficult thing to address.

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:13.172
But in their criminal referrals,

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:14.140
you remember

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:15.174
there was language

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:16.976
about the incitement statute.

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:18.978
They were trying to articulate

00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:20.213
the language tracks.

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:22.382
In some respects, the same outcome.

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:26.586
Was that a way to try to nudge the DOJ

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:28.721
to look into a statute

00:06:28.955 --> 00:06:30.590
that would do this very thing?

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:32.692
It was politically unavailable

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:33.993
to them, to the impeachment

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:36.629
I think

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:40.032
100%, although they also want to do it

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:40.900
on their own.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:42.702
It's sparse

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:43.970
precedent there,

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.571
but the last time it was done,

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:47.440
it was by vote of Congress.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.076
There is a 19th century precedent

00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:52.111
that says it has to be vote by Congress.

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:54.046
They want to do it both ways.

00:06:54.046 --> 00:06:56.582
They want to do belt and suspenders.

00:06:56.783 --> 00:06:58.184
And there's all kinds

00:06:58.184 --> 00:06:59.519
of different reasons why.

00:06:59.519 --> 00:07:01.621
It's tricky, I think, to remember

00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:03.489
this is a qualification.

00:07:03.489 --> 00:07:04.724
There are only three or four

00:07:04.724 --> 00:07:05.992
in the Constitution.

00:07:05.992 --> 00:07:08.060
You have to be 35 years old.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.629
You can't be foreign born

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:11.798
and you may not have done this.

00:07:11.798 --> 00:07:13.833
Now, the Supreme Court has made it clear

00:07:13.833 --> 00:07:16.068
you can't add to those qualifications.

00:07:16.269 --> 00:07:18.471
But if you're doing that insurrection,

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:19.338
that's what

00:07:19.338 --> 00:07:22.442
tracks that the 14th Amendment

00:07:22.742 --> 00:07:25.178
and it's a hard charge law.

00:07:25.211 --> 00:07:26.045
As you know.

00:07:26.045 --> 00:07:27.713
It's got a checkered history.

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:29.882
We have Trump saying be peaceful.

00:07:30.016 --> 00:07:31.684
There's no real agreement here.

00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:33.753
They have to go by aiding and abetting.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:34.887
That's why,

00:07:34.887 --> 00:07:35.755
though, I believe it

00:07:35.755 --> 00:07:38.291
showed up in this final list before that.

00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:40.059
And it was the surprise

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:41.761
entry of the four.

00:07:41.761 --> 00:07:43.262
We're really going to see in

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:45.264
subsequent weeks how this plays out.

00:07:45.765 --> 00:07:47.200
I also want to address this

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.301
because, you know,

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:48.601
you and

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.136
I have had these conversations

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:50.770
over the better

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:51.671
part of

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:53.272
six years at this point

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:55.641
about the notion of subpoena power

00:07:55.842 --> 00:07:57.076
and what would it

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:58.544
what it takes as prosecutor

00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:00.079
when you give somebody a subpoena.

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:01.180
It's not like an invitation

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.282
to a Tea Party. You can sort of decline.

00:08:03.516 --> 00:08:04.650
You're supposed to show up.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.352
But then in recent years,

00:08:06.352 --> 00:08:07.053
the subpoenas

00:08:07.053 --> 00:08:08.821
that have been issued by Congress,

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:09.555
they've had

00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:11.791
their noses have been thumbed at Congress

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:12.859
for a variety of reasons.

00:08:12.859 --> 00:08:14.227
One other recommendations

00:08:14.227 --> 00:08:15.795
in this very report

00:08:15.795 --> 00:08:17.563
deals with the House's civil

00:08:17.563 --> 00:08:19.632
subpoena enforcement authority.

00:08:19.932 --> 00:08:22.134
And they say the current authority

00:08:22.134 --> 00:08:24.437
on how to enforce their own subpoenas

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:27.507
through civil litigation is unclear.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:29.308
This is a really interesting

00:08:29.308 --> 00:08:31.377
take because they probably anticipate

00:08:31.644 --> 00:08:32.545
the fact that there has been

00:08:32.545 --> 00:08:34.847
and the Supreme Court then bets it down.

00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:35.181
about people no longer

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:36.649
seeing the gravitas

00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:38.050
that ought to be attached

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:39.352
to a congressional subpoena.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:42.255
It's a great point.

00:08:42.255 --> 00:08:43.089
And really, it's

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:45.091
the same with it's gotten comedy.

00:08:45.224 --> 00:08:46.058
Well, you can ignore

00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:48.294
congressional says the only reason

00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:50.663
the only reason is Congress's

00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:52.231
only last two years.

00:08:52.231 --> 00:08:55.134
And then once they expire, a subpoena

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:57.537
expired, while Trump in particular

00:08:57.637 --> 00:08:59.272
and before said go,

00:08:59.272 --> 00:09:00.206
it was always either

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:01.741
honored or negotiated

00:09:01.741 --> 00:09:02.642
But they develop

00:09:02.642 --> 00:09:03.543
this strategy

00:09:03.543 --> 00:09:05.912
of just playing it out until Congress.

00:09:06.746 --> 00:09:07.446
So you're right,

00:09:07.446 --> 00:09:10.283
it is one of the big recommendations.

00:09:10.283 --> 00:09:12.184
